questions.solution:
For the forward reaction of $A \rightleftharpoons B,
\begin{aligned}
\ln K & =-\frac{\Delta H}{R T} \quad \text{ [van't Hoff isotherm equation] } \\
K & =e^{-\Delta H / R T} \\
& =e^{\frac{-(-41.5)}{8.314 \times 10^{-3} \times 500}}=e^{9.983} \simeq e^{10}
\end{aligned}[\because \Delta H=$ Enthalpy change of the forward reaction $=-41.5 \mathrm{ kJ} \mathrm{ mol}^{-1}R=8.314 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{ kJ} \mathrm{ mol}^{-1} \mathrm{ K}^{-1}, T=500 \mathrm{ K}K=$ Equilibrium constant $]$

We know that $\begin{aligned}
\log\frac{k_2}{k_1}$& =$\frac{E_a}{2.303 R} \times \frac{10}{93 \times 1000}$\\
E_a & =0.3010 $\times2 \times93 \times100 \times2.303 \\
E_a \text{$ and $} =5598.6 \mathrm{cal} \times2.303 \\
E_a \text{ and } =56 \times2.303=12.89 \mathrm{kcal}$/ $\mathrm{mol}
\end{aligned}$

Since, concentration of the reactant decreases from $0.6 \mathrm{M}$ to $0.3 \mathrm{M}$ (i.e., halved) in 15 minutes, therefore half time for this reaction be $15 \mathrm{ min}$. $\text{ i.e. },$ t_{1 / 2}=15 $\mathrm{ min}$ Now for a first order reaction k=$\frac{0.693}{t_{1 / 2}}=\frac{0.693}{15} \mathrm{ min}^{-1}$ Again, $\quad k=\frac{2.303}{t} \log \frac{[A]_0}{[A]} \begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad\frac{0.693}{15}$& =$\frac{2.303}{t} \log \frac{0.1}{0.025}$\\
t & =$\frac{2.303 \times 15}{0.693} \times \log4 \\ \text{$ and $} =\frac{2.303 \times 15}{0.693} \times0.6020=30 \mathrm{ min}
\end{aligned}$ Alternate method
$\because$ Reaction is of first order with $t_{1 / 2}=15 \mathrm{ min} \begin{aligned}
\therefore0.1 \mathrm{M} \stackrel{15 \mathrm{ min}}{\longrightarrow} \frac{0.1}{2}$& =0.05 $\mathrm{M} \stackrel{15 \mathrm{ min}}{\longrightarrow} \frac{0.05}{2}$\\
& =0.025 $\mathrm{M}
\end{aligned}\therefore$ Total time $=15+15=30 \mathrm{ min}$
